window.docContents[127].resolve({"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___1___-What-is-representation-theory___/Irreducible-and-indecomposable-representations_SEMI_-typical-problems-of-representation-theory/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___1___-What-is-representation-theory___--Irreducible-and-indecomposable-representations_SEMI_-typical-problems-of-representation-theory--Irreducible-and-indecomposable-representations_SEMI_-typical-problems-of-representation-theory--Formalization--Primary-declarations":{"contents":"The auxiliary predicate excludes two nonbottom objects whose supremum is top and infimum is bottom.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Discussion_2.1_irreducible_indecomposable/Derived2; role=primary]\n\nThe enclosing auxiliary predicate holds for every simple module.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Discussion_2.1_irreducible_indecomposable; role=primary]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Discussion_2.1_irreducible_indecomposable/Derived01; role=primary]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Discussion_after_Theorem2.1.2; role=primary]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Discussion_irreducible_vs_indecomposable; role=primary]\n\nThe size-two Jordan block with parameter zero is indecomposable but not simple.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Discussion_2.1_irreducible_indecomposable/Derived4; role=primary]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Discussion_irreducible_vs_indecomposable/Derived2; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Example2.3.14; role=primary]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.1. What is representation theory?\u0009Irreducible and indecomposable representations; typical problems of representation theory\u0009Irreducible and indecomposable representations; typical problems of representation theory\u0009Formalization","header":"Primary declarations","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___1___-What-is-representation-theory___/Irreducible-and-indecomposable-representations_SEMI_-typical-problems-of-representation-theory/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___1___-What-is-representation-theory___--Irreducible-and-indecomposable-representations_SEMI_-typical-problems-of-representation-theory--Irreducible-and-indecomposable-representations_SEMI_-typical-problems-of-representation-theory--Formalization--Primary-declarations"},"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___3___-Representations/Subrepresentation/#Chapter2___Definition2___3___4":{"contents":"Definition 2.3.4. A subrepresentation of a representation V of an algebra A is a subspace W \\subset V which is invariant under all the operators \\rho(a) : V \\to V, a \\in A.\n\nFor instance, 0 and V are always subrepresentations.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.3. Representations\u0009Subrepresentation","header":"Subrepresentation","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___3___-Representations/Subrepresentation/#Chapter2___Definition2___3___4"},"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___9___-Lie-algebras/Basis-dependent-definition-of-universal-enveloping-algebra/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___9___-Lie-algebras--Basis-dependent-definition-of-universal-enveloping-algebra":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.9. Lie algebras","header":"Basis-dependent definition of universal enveloping algebra","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___9___-Lie-algebras/Basis-dependent-definition-of-universal-enveloping-algebra/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___9___-Lie-algebras--Basis-dependent-definition-of-universal-enveloping-algebra"},"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___9___-Lie-algebras/Derivations-as-infinitesimal-automorphisms_SEMI_-motivation-for-Lie-algebras/#Chapter2___Remark2___9___4":{"contents":"Remark 2.9.4. Derivations are important because they are the \"infinitesimal version\" of automorphisms (i.e., isomorphisms onto itself). For example, assume that g(t) is a differentiable family of automorphisms of a finite dimensional algebra A over \\mathbb{R} or \\mathbb{C} parametrized by t \\in (-\\epsilon, \\epsilon) such that g(0) = \\mathrm{Id}. Then D := g'(0) : A \\to A is a derivation (check it!). Conversely, if D : A \\to A is a derivation, then e^{tD} is a 1-parameter family of automorphisms (give a proof!).\n\nThis provides a motivation for the notion of a Lie algebra. Namely, we see that Lie algebras arise as spaces of infinitesimal automorphisms (= derivations) of associative algebras. In fact, they similarly arise as spaces of derivations of any kind of linear algebraic structures, such as Lie algebras, Hopf algebras, etc., and for this reason play a very important role in algebra.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.9. Lie algebras\u0009Derivations as infinitesimal automorphisms; motivation for Lie algebras","header":"Derivations as infinitesimal automorphisms; motivation for Lie algebras","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___9___-Lie-algebras/Derivations-as-infinitesimal-automorphisms_SEMI_-motivation-for-Lie-algebras/#Chapter2___Remark2___9___4"},"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___1___-Subrepresentations-in-semisimple-representations/Generalization-of-Proposition-3___1___4-to-non-algebraically-closed-fields/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--3___-General-results-of-representation-theory--3___1___-Subrepresentations-in-semisimple-representations--Generalization-of-Proposition-3___1___4-to-non-algebraically-closed-fields--Generalization-of-Proposition-3___1___4-to-non-algebraically-closed-fields--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"For finite function modules over a simple module, injectivity is characterized by the displayed matrix relation.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Remark3.1.5; role=supporting]\n\nLinear independence of a matrix of endomorphisms is characterized by the displayed matrix relation.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Remark3.1.5; role=supporting]\n\nAn additive equivalence between linear maps of finite function modules and matrices of module endomorphisms.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Remark3.1.5; role=supporting]\n\nA matrix entry of a linear map is its value on the corresponding single-coordinate input.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Remark3.1.5; role=supporting]\n\nThe endomorphism ring of a simple module admits a division ring structure.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Remark3.1.5; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00093. General results of representation theory\u00093.1. Subrepresentations in semisimple representations\u0009Generalization of Proposition 3.1.4 to non-algebraically-closed fields\u0009Generalization of Proposition 3.1.4 to non-algebraically-closed fields\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___1___-Subrepresentations-in-semisimple-representations/Generalization-of-Proposition-3___1___4-to-non-algebraically-closed-fields/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--3___-General-results-of-representation-theory--3___1___-Subrepresentations-in-semisimple-representations--Generalization-of-Proposition-3___1___4-to-non-algebraically-closed-fields--Generalization-of-Proposition-3___1___4-to-non-algebraically-closed-fields--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___13___-Proof-of-the-classification-theorem-for-representations-of-___S_n___/a_lambda-C_LSQ_S_n_RSQ_-b_mu-___-0-when-lambda-_GT_-mu-lexicographically/#Chapter5___Lemma5___13___2":{"contents":"Lemma 5.13.2. If \\lambda > \\mu, then a_\\lambda \\mathbb{C}[S_n] b_\\mu = 0.\n\nProof. Similarly to the previous lemma, it suffices to show that for any g \\in S_n there exists a transposition t \\in P_\\lambda such that g^{-1}tg \\in Q_\\mu. Let T = T_\\lambda and T' = gT_\\mu. We claim that there are two integers which are in the same row of T and the same column of T'. Indeed, if \\lambda_1 > \\mu_1, this is clear by the pigeonhole principle (already for the first row). Otherwise, if \\lambda_1 = \\mu_1, as in the proof of the previous lemma, we can find elements p_1 \\in P_\\lambda, q'_1 \\in gQ_\\mu g^{-1} such that p_1 T and q'_1 T' have the same first row and repeat the argument for the second row, and so on. Eventually, having done i-1 such steps, we'll have \\lambda_i > \\mu_i, which means that some two elements of the ith row of the first tableau are in the same column of the second tableau, completing the proof. \\square\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.13. Proof of the classification theorem for representations of $S_n$\u0009a_lambda C[S_n] b_mu = 0 when lambda > mu lexicographically","header":"a_lambda C[S_n] b_mu = 0 when lambda > mu lexicographically","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___13___-Proof-of-the-classification-theorem-for-representations-of-___S_n___/a_lambda-C_LSQ_S_n_RSQ_-b_mu-___-0-when-lambda-_GT_-mu-lexicographically/#Chapter5___Lemma5___13___2"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___15___-The-Frobenius-character-formula/Continuation-of-proof-of-the-Frobenius-character-formula-_LPAR_from-missing-page_RPAR_/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___15___-The-Frobenius-character-formula--Continuation-of-proof-of-the-Frobenius-character-formula-_LPAR_from-missing-page_RPAR_":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.15. The Frobenius character formula","header":"Continuation of proof of the Frobenius character formula (from missing page)","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___15___-The-Frobenius-character-formula/Continuation-of-proof-of-the-Frobenius-character-formula-_LPAR_from-missing-page_RPAR_/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___15___-The-Frobenius-character-formula--Continuation-of-proof-of-the-Frobenius-character-formula-_LPAR_from-missing-page_RPAR_"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___18___-Schur-Weyl-duality-for-______mathfrak___gl____LPAR_V_RPAR____/Schur-Weyl-duality___-V_________n___-___-___-V_lambda-___-L_lambda/#Chapter5___Theorem5___18___4___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.18. Schur-Weyl duality for $\\mathfrak{gl}(V)$\u0009Schur-Weyl duality: V^{⊗n} = ⊕ V_lambda ⊗ L_lambda\u0009Schur-Weyl duality: V^{⊗n} = ⊕ V_lambda ⊗ L_lambda","header":"Formalization","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___18___-Schur-Weyl-duality-for-______mathfrak___gl____LPAR_V_RPAR____/Schur-Weyl-duality___-V_________n___-___-___-V_lambda-___-L_lambda/#Chapter5___Theorem5___18___4___formalization"},"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___2___-Functors/Examples-of-functors/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--7___-Introduction-to-categories--7___2___-Functors--Examples-of-functors":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00097. Introduction to categories\u00097.2. Functors","header":"Examples of functors","id":"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___2___-Functors/Examples-of-functors/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--7___-Introduction-to-categories--7___2___-Functors--Examples-of-functors"},"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___6___-Adjoint-functors/Motivation-for-adjoint-functor-terminology/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--7___-Introduction-to-categories--7___6___-Adjoint-functors--Motivation-for-adjoint-functor-terminology":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00097. Introduction to categories\u00097.6. Adjoint functors","header":"Motivation for adjoint functor terminology","id":"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___6___-Adjoint-functors/Motivation-for-adjoint-functor-terminology/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--7___-Introduction-to-categories--7___6___-Adjoint-functors--Motivation-for-adjoint-functor-terminology"},"/8___-Homological-algebra/8___2___-Tor-and-Ext-functors/Projective-resolution/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--8___-Homological-algebra--8___2___-Tor-and-Ext-functors--Projective-resolution":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00098. Homological algebra\u00098.2. Tor and Ext functors","header":"Projective resolution","id":"/8___-Homological-algebra/8___2___-Tor-and-Ext-functors/Projective-resolution/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--8___-Homological-algebra--8___2___-Tor-and-Ext-functors--Projective-resolution"}});